# **cmd.exe reads a batch file by byte offset.** With LF endings the position drifts by one per
# line and it starts running from the middle of them, so every line loses its first few characters
# and the errors name fragments of words. That is what a handover of this file did the first time.
#
# `-text` rather than `text eol=crlf` on purpose. `eol=crlf` normalises to LF *in the repository*
# and converts on checkout, which is right for a clone and wrong for the way this file actually
# travels: downloaded straight from the web, where the stored LF bytes are what arrives. `-text`
# stores the CRLF bytes verbatim, so a download and a checkout give the same working file.
*.bat -text
*.ps1 -text

# Everything a shell reads stays LF, whatever the local git config says.
*.sh text eol=lf
